The Dining Hall
An enormous domed hall of white linen and polished brass, under a ceiling that
has been painted with the night sky and then, at some point, switched on. Tables
run off into a golden distance. On a small stage at the far end, a band is
playing something faintly apologetic. Your own table, round and large and
beautifully laid, stands in the middle. The foyer is south, the kitchen east
through a swing door, and glass doors to the north open onto a terrace.

Tricia McMillan is here, watching everyone with a journalist's patient eye. 

Random lounges against the wall with her arms folded, looking at everything the
way a cat looks at a bath. 

Ford is here, restless, taking in the place as though pricing it. 

Marvin stands beside your chair with a tray, in the way of someone who has been
waiting a very long time for no one to ask. 

Random, who has not said a word since the pop, reaches into her coat and hands
you something folded very small. "I took it from your hut," she says, not
looking at you. "On the first day. I wanted to know what you looked at." It is
the pencil sketch. She has smoothed it flat.

[The score has just gone up by three hundred and sixty points.]

