Time passes.

The kitchen door swings, and a small dented robot puts his head round it. "I
heard a noise," he says. "A really sad one. I came to see."

Floyd sees Marvin and stops dead, with one foot still in the air. Marvin sees
Floyd, and does not move at all, which for Marvin is a form of surprise.

"Oh boy," says Floyd, softly. "You're big. I mean, you're sad. You're very, very
sad. That is the best sad I have ever seen."

Marvin turns his head, by perhaps two degrees. "I have a brain the size of a
planet," he says, "and in all of it I had not allowed for this." A pause that
lasts about a century. "You are cheerful. I have not seen cheerful since the
Heart of Gold. It did not end well."

"I don't mind," says Floyd. "I'll just stand here and be cheerful, and you can
stand there and be sad, and nobody has to do anything clever. I'm good at that.
Can I carry your tray?"

Marvin looks at the tray for some time. "Nobody has ever asked," he says. He
does not hand it over. But he tilts it, very slightly, towards Floyd, and Floyd
takes one corner of it with enormous care, and they stand there like that.

>z
Time passes.

>ask marvin about floyd
"Floyd," says Marvin, as if testing the word for a crack. "He is a very small
robot with a very large capacity for being pleased. I have told him that all of
it is pointless. He said thank you. I do not know what to do with a person like
that." A pause. "I find I do not want him to stop."

>ask floyd about marvin
"Marvin," says Floyd, in the voice of someone who has been handed a very rare
thing. "He's the saddest person I ever met. I'm going to look after him. He says
it won't help. I said I know, but I'll do it anyway."

>examine floyd
Floyd stands at Marvin's elbow with one corner of the tray in both hands,
looking at the world with undiminished delight. Marvin looks at it with
undiminished despair. Between them they seem to have the whole of it covered.

>n

The Observation Terrace
A long glass terrace running along the whole curve of the building, open to a
sky that is not a sky: the Universe, in its last hours, spilling slowly from the
end of the dining hall like a great slow fire. The corner table is set for six.
The dining hall is south.

On the corner table is a pencil.

Fenchurch sits at the corner table, alone, looking out at the end of the
Universe. 

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

Tricia follows, taking in the place with a journalist's eye.

Random drifts in after you at a distance she clearly considers dignified.

Floyd trots in after you, one hand still on the tray.

>examine fenchurch
She sits at the corner table with her chin on her hand, looking at the end of
the Universe with the look of someone who has come a long way to be looking at
it with someone. Her head tilts, just so: to the left, and slightly back, and
with a small pause at the top as if the world were a question she meant to
answer later. You have drawn that tilt forty times. It is nothing like the
drawing.

>take pencil
(the pencil)
Taken.

>draw
You smooth the sketch on the corner table and look at her, properly, as you have
not let yourself look in a very long time. Then you draw: not what you remember,
not what you wished, but the angle of her head, the small pause at the top, and
the smile that follows it by exactly the time it takes to decide to. It takes a
minute. When you look up, Fenchurch is watching your hands. You turn the sketch
round. She looks at it for a long time. "There," she says, quietly. "That's it.
That's me."

[The score has just gone up by twelve points.]

>toast
You stand, which is not usual, and pick up a glass, which is. The table looks
up: Fenchurch, with her head on one side, Random, with her chin; Ford, in the
middle of an anecdote he abandons; Tricia, with a journalist's raised eyebrow;
Floyd, holding a glass he has been given, entirely sincerely upside down; and
you find you have nothing prepared.

"To the end of the Universe," you say, "which I am told does this every night,
and to everyone who turned up for it." You look at Random, who looks at her
plate. "To the people who were late. And to the ones who were never going to be
anywhere else."

The glasses touch. Beyond the glass, the Universe finishes, magnificently, the
way it does every night; and at your table nobody is looking at it, because
everyone is looking at each other.

The cube is nowhere. The Vogons will have to find some other way. For now the
Earth is a little further away and entirely, for the moment, intact; Random has
her father's chin and she is, with an effort, willing to be seen with it. The
universe ends, over the soup, and you find that you do not mind. It is, on the
whole, mostly harmless.

[The score has just gone up by eleven points.]


